WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.420
Ad Astra sends Brad Pitt to the far reaches
of space on a quest for his father, the truth,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:06.880
and a sense of deeper meaning.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.720
If you're wondering what it all adds up to,
let's break down the ending and major takeaways

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:11.720
of Ad Astra.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.280
After SpaceCom determines that Clifford McBride
is likely behind the destructive antimatter

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.430
pulse that kicks off the movie's plot, his
son Roy is dispatched on a mission to Mars

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.740
in an effort to establish communication with
the elder McBride.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.560
Roy ends up journeying all the way to Neptune,
where he discovers that SpaceCom was right,

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:33.290
his father is still alive, and was at least
partly responsible for the antimatter surge.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.890
The surge came after the crew of his station
staged a deadly mutiny, irrevocably damaging

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.560
the battery powering the station.

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.820
But the true tragedy, at least in Clifford's
eyes, is the result of all their research,

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.660
which shows no sign of intelligent alien life
in the universe.

00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:51.340
Although the Lima Project compiled reams of
data about outlying planets and systems, it

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.440
didn't find what Clifford was really looking
for.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:57.160
Clifford was so determined to find new life
that he was willing to abandon his family

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.360
and even kill for it.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:02.280
Although the information he did gather is
valuable, for him, it's not enough.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.840
Roy McBride is uniquely qualified for the
mission to determine the fate of Project Lima.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.800
There's his family connection to the Project's
long-lost leader, and the fact that he is,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.660
like Neil Armstrong was described to be, an
almost supernaturally reserved man.

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:19.740
Even working in dangerous conditions in deep
space, his pulse goes no higher than 80bpm,

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.480
and he deals with the bad news about his father
with a steely calm.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.480
"Now, we're counting on you to help us find
him.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.200
Are you with us?"

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.500
"Yes I am, sir."

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.380
It's this quality that comes in extra handy
when Roy realizes what SpaceCom is really

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:40.320
up to, specifically, the agency is out to
double-cross him and kill Clifford.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.280
The secret plan all along is to observe Roy's
attempt to establish communication with Clifford,

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.780
using Clifford's response to figure out where
he is, and then travel there to finish him

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.260
off with a nuclear weapon.

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:55.320
Once he figures it out, Roy proves more than
a match for the other team, successfully sabotaging

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.900
their mission before journeying to Neptune
in peace.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:03.100
The central message of Ad Astra is best summed
up by Roy's words to his father just before

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:04.220
they leave Project Lima.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.420
Roy tells his father that what Cliff has proven,
that there's no alien life to be found, is

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:11.560
just as important as finding another world
full of intelligence.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:15.200
By learning there is no intelligent life in
the known universe, Cliff has shown us that,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.330
as Roy puts it, "We're all we've got."

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:21.830
Speaking to Digital Spy, writer/director James
Gray explained that the idea of looking for

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.040
answers in alien life isn't a particularly
appealing idea to him.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:25.700
Gray said,

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.340
"The idea that there's these little green
men out there that'll either save us or eat

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:32.760
us, to me that's more horrifying than having
to rely on other people."

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:37.940
With the Lima Project's antimatter battery
damaged, the station is a lost cause, actively

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.540
endangering life on other planets as it breaks
down.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.880
Clifford can't repair it, but thankfully,
Roy has arrived with a solution of sorts:

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.420
the nuclear bomb SpaceCom intended to use
on Clifford.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:51.920
After getting his father out of the station,
Roy uses the bomb for part of its intended

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.700
purpose, blowing up the station and getting
rid of the battery.

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:58.960
It's an ignominious end for Lima Project,
which set out on a hopeful mission before

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:02.880
spiraling into madness, violence, and catastrophic
loss of life.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:07.600
Clifford ran away from Earth hoping to find
answers, but ended up creating more problems,

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:11.319
underlining one of the movie's major themes:
running away from problems we're meant to

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.580
face head-on can only lead to disappointment.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.740
Somewhere between Earth and Neptune, it seems
pretty obvious that Cliff McBride has lost

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:18.740
his freaking mind.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:22.430
But one of the interesting things about Ad
Astra is that with Roy's voyage, we get to

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.920
follow in Cliff's footsteps and see exactly
how his marbles spilled out.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:29.700
Roy's narration tells us there are physical
strains affecting his mental health, like

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:31.760
the extended time spent in zero gravity.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.280
There's also the crushing isolation of space,
something Roy commits to facing as he continues

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:40.350
on his quest to find his father, going past
the moon, past Mars, and then all the way

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:41.300
to Neptune.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.240
"Are you ready?"

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:45.720
"I'm ready."

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:50.140
Roy's journey mirrors what must have been
his father's in a lot of ways, including the

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:50.940
death toll.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.900
When Roy reaches his father, we learn the
antimatter surges aren't something Cliff has

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.040
been causing on purpose.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.900
His struggles against his crew, he says, caused
the damage that led to the antimatter instability.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.580
This is something Roy is familiar with, because
his trek through the stars has left its own

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.870
trail of collateral damage; with few exceptions,
just about everyone who has been at Roy's

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:12.040
side throughout his journey is dead by the
end of the movie.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.820
Though he doesn't mean for it to happen, he
indirectly causes the death of the surviving

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:20.150
crew on the ship to Neptune, just as Cliff
indirectly unleashes the antimatter surge

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:21.820
that threatens the solar system.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.880
After they escape Project Lima, Cliff detaches
himself from Roy, guaranteeing his own certain

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:27.680
death.

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:29.840
He has more than a few reasons for doing it.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:33.100
First, there are the consequences he might
have to face upon returning to Earth.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.680
He doesn't know that his crimes have been
swept under the rug, and so has no reason

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.300
to think he'll be shown any mercy.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:42.740
Second, while he may refuse to admit it to
himself, what he calls his destiny has proven

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:43.620
to be a sham.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.480
Considering everything he's done for that
"destiny," how could he not want to end things?

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.640
Finally, as he tells Roy, this lonely void
has become his home.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:52.580
He has no desire to return to Earth.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:56.880
Ad Astra has been compared to a number of
classic movies, from the awe and wonder of

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.480
2001: A Space Odyssey, the character study
of First Man, and the grounded thrills of

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.380
Gravity.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.760
It's also drawn comparisons to Apocalypse
Now, in the way Roy is drawn deeper and deeper

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.580
into space on his dangerous quest.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:13.000
But Ad Astra is fundamentally about the desire
for connection between parents and their offspring,

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.680
and between people in general, and the dark
places people can be driven to when that connection

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.680
is lost.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.320
"It's a very human story dealing with our
connection to humanity."

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:27.380
In many ways, Roy McBride's journey is a terribly
sad one, he's betrayed by his employers, and

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:32.120
faced with the reality of his father's crimes,
potential insanity, and then death.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.260
He loses the parent he journeyed billions
of miles to save.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.320
But in spite of that loss, the movie's message
remains hopeful.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.260
As Roy tells Clifford, we're all we've got,
and those bonds are strong enough to take

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:44.120
us all to Neptune and back.

